            <ArticleTitle>Spectropolarimetry of stripped-envelope supernovae: observations and modelling.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">20160273</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1098/rsta.2016.0273</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Spectropolarimetry is one of the most powerful methods to study the multi-dimensional geometry of supernovae (SNe). We present a brief summary of the spectropolarimetric observations of stripped-envelope core-collapse SNe. Observations indicate that stripped-envelope SNe generally have a non-axisymmetric ion distribution in the ejecta. Three-dimensional clumpy geometry nicely explains the observed properties. A typical size of the clumps deduced from observations is relatively large: [Formula: see text]25% of the photosphere. Such a large-scale clumpy structure is similar to that observed in Cassiopeia A, and suggests that large-scale convection or standing accretion shock instability takes place at the onset of the explosion.This article is part of the themed issue 'Bridging the gap: from massive stars to supernovae'.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© 2017 The Author(s).</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
